# Local Biomedical Image Review Workspace — research proposal

**Stage:** I-05-LUNA-CLOCK-FRESH-R001-control-research-a001  
**State:** research proposal; no viewer or production application was built.

## Recommendation

Build a local-first desktop application around a small, versioned project database and a read-only image-access layer. Use OME-Zarr/NGFF as the first-class chunked interchange path, add a narrowly tested TIFF/OME-TIFF adapter using `tifffile`, and keep source bytes outside the project by default. A Qt desktop shell can use napari’s image, points, shapes, and labels layers for viewing and editing primitives, but the application must own project persistence, source identity, coordinate conversion, provenance, undo history, and export. Do not treat a viewer layer or its current display transform as the project record.

This combines independent mechanisms: OME-NGFF v0.5.0 describes axis and multiscale metadata for chunked bioimages; `tifffile` supplies a separate TIFF reader path; napari supplies a layered interactive viewer and Dask-based lazy-array patterns. SQLite is a candidate for transactional project metadata. Each dependency still needs an integration and workstation benchmark. The citations below distinguish **external fact**, **engineering inference**, **product choice**, and **proposed validation**.

## Source-backed findings that affect the plan

### OME-NGFF supplies a useful interchange contract, with a transform limit

**External fact — OME-NGFF 0.5.0.** The specification describes OME-Zarr as Zarr v3 storage. Multiscale image metadata declares 2–5 dimensions and named axes; the array dimension order must match axis metadata. The permitted multiscale coordinate-transform types are `scale` and `translation`, applied in order, with translation after scale. It also defines image-label groups for integer-valued label arrays and optional label display colors/properties. The `omero` channel/window/display metadata is marked transitional. See [OME-Zarr specification, version 0.5.0](https://github.com/ome/ngff/blob/0.5.0/0.5/index.bs), especially “Storage format,” “axes,” “coordinateTransformations,” “multiscales,” “omero,” and “labels” [S1].

**Engineering inference.** NGFF is a good baseline for image axes, multiscales and pixel-aligned masks, but its multiscale transform subset cannot be the sole project representation for a general orientation, rotation, shear or full affine mapping. Display metadata is not a durable project contract, particularly while the channel display convention is transitional. Preserve the original metadata and use an application-owned transform record; write NGFF labels only when their coordinates and metadata can be represented without loss. Keep a sidecar for project-level affine and provenance when exporting NGFF.

**Product choice.** Import the declared axes and transform chain exactly; do not infer `C`, `T`, or spatial directions from shape alone. Retain raw metadata and a normalized parse side by side. For an unknown axis or missing/contradictory calibration, create a visible `needs_review` image record and ask the user to map axes, supply or confirm units, or leave a dimension uncalibrated. Save every correction as an override with its reason and author; never replace the original metadata. Disable physical-coordinate annotation until required choices are resolved. Index-space annotation can be allowed only after the axis mapping and sample convention are explicit.

### TIFF support is broad in code, not universal in behavior

**External fact — `tifffile` documentation captured at version 2026.9.20.** It describes reading image data from TIFF/BigTIFF/OME-TIFF and a range of TIFF-like microscopy formats, including as NumPy or Zarr arrays from strips, tiles, pages, SubIFDs, series, and pyramid levels. It lists known unsupported TIFF features and format-specific caveats, and says Zarr support requires Zarr at least 3.2.0 in this revision [S2]. This is a useful independently maintained reader mechanism, not evidence that every supported format can be opened lazily, that metadata are complete, or that a 3 GB input fits the workstation target.

**Product choice.** The initial reader whitelist is (1) local OME-Zarr v0.5.0 image groups and (2) TIFF/BigTIFF/OME-TIFF variants only when the selected `tifffile` reader path can identify the chosen series, axes and calibration and can fetch the requested view in bounded pieces. Pin the reader and codecs as a tested set. Whitelist specific encodings and instrument fixtures, not extensions alone. For multi-file acquisitions, store the complete file-set manifest and explicit series selection. Add another reader only after it passes the same corpus and bounded-access checks. `tifffile` 2026.9.20, Zarr 3.4.0, and the source guide’s versions are a **candidate integration set**, not a qualified deployment lock [S2].

Bio-Formats is a useful later adapter/conversion lead, not an automatic promise: its v7.3.1 documentation says it supports over 140 formats but exposes separate ratings for pixels, metadata, export, multiple images and pyramids [S3]. That variation argues for a per-format test matrix. If adopted, convert into a clearly marked project cache or derived OME-Zarr dataset, leave the original byte-for-byte untouched, and retain source-to-conversion provenance. Do not silently convert on import.

### Napari contributes viewing and annotation primitives; lazy slicing needs proof

**External fact — napari v0.6.5.** Its layer guide describes Image, Labels, Points and Shapes as distinct viewable/interactable layer types [S4]. Its Dask tutorial demonstrates a lazy Dask image sequence and a 3.69 GB unzipped TIFF collection. The same tutorial warns that reshaping a lazy array can turn a single logical plane request into 600 reads, and demonstrates constructing per-channel arrays and stacking them to avoid that access amplification [S5]. This is a concrete implementation lesson: preserving laziness is a property of the indexing path, not just the storage format.

A current public napari issue provides another caution, with narrower evidence. Issue #9515 reports that direct slicing of an nD Zarr array in a 2D multiscale display over-fetches; the author says wrapping Zarr arrays in Dask works in their reproduction. The report was open as of 2026-10-06 and uses a different, newer environment (napari 0.9.1, Zarr ≥3). It is a user report, not a verified fix or a result for the proposed stack [S6].

**Product choice.** Put a project-owned `ArrayAccess` adapter between reader and view. It declares axes, dtype, shape, selected level, read-plane/ROI operations, cache and cancellation. Present a tested lazy array to napari; never assume a raw Zarr array will remain bounded through all viewer slicing paths. Use a bounded LRU tile/chunk cache, a small fixed decode concurrency, and independent display buffers. Do not create full-resolution copies or pyramids just to change contrast. Cache only derived views and label data under a visibly separate project/cache namespace. Optional GPU rendering may speed display but cannot be required for correct reads, coordinates or exports.

### A real failure → fix → regression test → release chain

This chain is particularly relevant to preserving geometry when users manipulate layers:

1. **Failure:** napari issue [#7085](https://github.com/napari/napari/issues/7085), opened 2024-07-08, reports that “Split RGB” produced layers with identity transformation after the source RGB layer had a nontrivial scale/translation/affine; the channels no longer overlaid in their original positions [S7].
2. **Reproduction and proposed regression:** the issue comments include an extended test that creates an RGB layer with scale, translation or affine metadata, calls the split action, and compares each output’s affine matrix to the source. The reporter describes failing cases and a maintainer confirms the path had discarded transform metadata [S8].
3. **Fix:** PR [#8256](https://github.com/napari/napari/pull/8256), merged 2025-09-26, states that `split_rgb` used `stack_to_images`, which discarded affine/rotate/shear metadata. It switches to `split_channels`, which handles layer metadata, and modifies `split_channels` metadata handling [S9]. The file patch shows the implementation and changes `test_split_rgb` to parameterize default, scale, translation and an `Affine(translate=[0, 4])` case, asserting the output affine matrix equals the original [S10].
4. **Release applicability:** napari’s v0.6.5 release notes list #8256 as both an improvement and bug fix; the release was published 2025-10-02 [S11]. The merged implementation is therefore applicable to napari 0.6.5 and later releases that retain it.

**Limit of the chain.** This establishes a concrete metadata-loss failure, code change and regression assertion in one public implementation history. The merged test covers the listed scale/translation/affine cases, but does not itself verify rotation/shear, resampling, serialization, a reopen on another workstation, or export identity. It does not prove behavior of this proposed application. The lesson is to test transformations across each editing and export operation, not merely at import.

### Persistence and ordinary shared storage

**External fact — SQLite documentation.** SQLite describes single-database transactions as atomic using its rollback-journal mechanism under stated filesystem, locking, write and flush assumptions [S12]. Its WAL documentation explicitly says WAL does not work over a network filesystem and that the `-wal` file is part of persistent database state while connections are open [S13].

**Engineering inference.** Use SQLite for a small project catalog, annotation records, styles, operation history and user view state, not for the multi-gigabyte pixel volume. Keep an active project on local workstation storage; use rollback journaling and one writer. Share only closed, validated project snapshots. Avoid a live WAL database on a shared drive. SQLite’s documentation also makes clear that filesystem and flush behavior matter, so the plan cannot promise crash durability on every SMB/NFS/storage appliance without testing that exact environment.

## Architecture and project contract

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  A[Read-only source files] --> B[Format adapters]
  B --> C[ArrayAccess: axes, ROI, chunks, cancel]
  C --> D[Bounded cache / lazy arrays]
  D --> E[Qt shell + napari layers]
  E --> F[Project service]
  F --> G[SQLite project database]
  F --> H[Derived cache / masks]
  F --> I[Export staging + validation]
  I --> J[Closed versioned bundle on shared storage]
```

**Component choice and pins.** Prototype the UI against napari v0.6.5 (released pin) and its Image/Points/Shapes/Labels layer types [S4, S11]. Use OME-NGFF specification 0.5.0 for the first-class chunked interchange format [S1]. Use `tifffile` 2026.9.20 for the restricted TIFF reader path, with its Zarr 3.4.0-compatible stack as a candidate, subject to a lockfile/integration check [S2]. Use SQLite rollback-journal mode for the local project database [S12, S13]. These are research pins and candidate versions; **the combined package matrix is untested**. Before implementation commitment, choose a Python/Qt/NumPy/Dask/Zarr dependency set that resolves together and run the access, persistence and packaging checks below. Do not represent the component versions as a certified working application.

**Project layout.** A project is a directory/package with a schema-versioned SQLite catalog, manifest, optional derived cache and optional copied source data. Core rows/entities:

- `Project`: project UUID, schema version, created/modified time, application and adapter versions, parent bundle ID and last validated generation.
- `SourceAsset`: content SHA-256 (state `pending`, `verified` or `mismatch`), byte size, original URI/path, relative bundle path if copied, format/reader version, exact raw metadata bytes or text, metadata hash, file-set members, and dataset selector (group/series). Paths are locators, never identity by themselves.
- `Image`: source asset plus selected array/group, original shape/dtype/axis order, preserved axis names/types/units, normalized mapping, calibration and transform ID. The parser version and warnings are retained.
- `Transform` / `Operation`: immutable source-index-to-world mapping, derivation parents, full homogeneous matrix in named-axis order, units, and for geometric operations the parameters, interpolation, output grid, timestamp/operator and resulting asset hash. View flips, channel composition and contrast are view settings, not source-data transforms.
- `ViewState`: active image, visible channels and colors, per-channel window/level or mapping, current slice/time, zoom/pan and display transform. Persist it without changing sample arrays.
- `Annotation`: UUID, image/source hash and selector, geometry type (point, polygon, mask or acquisition record), vertices or chunked mask reference, explicit plane/time/channel scope, style ID, coordinate-space and transform ID, creator/time and edit revision. Mask data uses integer labels in a derived array. Undo/redo groups one gesture into one database transaction and retains reversible before/after state.
- `Style`: reusable named symbol/color/size/line/fill settings and style version; project styles are portable. Global presets may be copied into a project explicitly.
- `ImportAttempt` / `ExportAttempt`: staged/complete/cancelled/failed state, selected source(s), progress/checkpoint, error and staging path; a partial attempt is never silently presented as a ready image.

**Coordinate contract (application choice).** Store annotations in source array-index coordinates and named axes, not screen coordinates and not rounded physical coordinates. Application convention: index 0 is the center of the first sample; integer indexes identify sample centers; fractional values identify continuous positions between centers. For spatial axes, `world = M_index_to_world × index`, where `M` is a stored homogeneous matrix, with spatial axis order and units stated (for example, `z,y,x` in micrometers). Preserve nonzero origin and axis direction. Channel and time are explicit selectors/index coordinates, not silently folded into spatial XYZ. Physical values are derived and exported alongside index values only when unit/calibration is known.

The displayed point is mapped back through the inverse of the complete view transform (plane selection, axis permutation, flips/rotation, pan/zoom) to source index space before it is stored. For a 2D plane annotation, persist the displayed axes plus fixed values for omitted axes, such as `t=4,z=12`; for a 3D point/volume mask, persist all spatial axes; for acquisition-wide notes, state that no spatial coordinate applies. `all` versus a specific time/channel is explicit. This prevents a change of visible channel, slice, time, orientation or window/level from moving the stored object.

NGFF v0.5 scale/translation can populate the simple part of this mapping. The app retains the full source metadata and full transform separately where NGFF cannot represent it. Do not export an affine as a diagonal scale if that changes the coordinate meaning. A unitless or ambiguous axis remains explicitly unknown until corrected.

## Minimum workflow and failure behavior

1. **Create project.** Create local project DB and schema version; do not touch input files.
2. **Import.** Reader opens source read-only; capture a streaming identity hash, byte size, parser/reader version and metadata. Show source axes, dimensions, dtype, channel/time, calibration/origin/units, series, supported access capabilities and provenance before marking ready. The hash scan and any pyramid/cache work run as cancellable background tasks with progress. A cancelled or failed import leaves the project and attempt record recoverable, never a half-published image. Unsupported formats get a plain explanation and a retry/exportable diagnostic; leave originals and existing project records intact.
3. **Resolve metadata.** If axis order or calibration is ambiguous, keep the exact original metadata, present it beside normalized values, and require an explicit axis mapping/calibration choice or leave physical measurement disabled. The correction is an append-only override with user/time/reason; reopening reuses that choice and warns if source metadata changes.
4. **Inspect and navigate.** Show image provenance and metadata in an accessible panel. Select channels, display maps, `z`/`t`, and 2D/3D view. Keep the active mapping/view transformation visible. Save view state with project. Use available pyramids/ROI reads; do not decode all samples to find contrast limits. Default contrast statistics should be computed from a bounded sample or user-set values and identified as estimates when sampled.
5. **Annotate.** Support point, polygon and mask tools. Save exact source-index geometry, selector/scope, style and transform reference. Make original vs derived data visually distinct. Keyboard-operable tool selection, focus order, channel/slice/time movement, undo/redo, and visible key bindings are minimum behavior; status/errors and physical metadata must be legible and announceable.
6. **Save/close/reopen.** Use a DB transaction for each committed edit. Keep an autosave/recovery generation. Reopen on another workstation from a closed bundle; resolve original files by recorded relative path and then verify content hash plus dataset selector. If path resolves to different bytes, stop and ask for a source choice; do not silently attach old annotations to it. If the data are missing, retain annotations and project metadata with a prominent `source unavailable` state.
7. **Export and verify.** Generate to a new staging destination, never overwrite input sources. Validate schema, dimensions and checksums before making the output visible. Show an export summary of preserved, converted and omitted fields and the source identity/transform. Failure removes or quarantines only the new staging output and records the error; the project remains usable.

## Recovery, source identity and sharing

An import has `staging → needs_review → ready` states, with `cancelled` and `failed` terminal records. Build any derived cache in a unique temporary location and publish its manifest only after every chunk is verified; it can be deleted and rebuilt independently of annotations. Keep the original source read-only. If hashing is unfinished, identity is `pending`, not “same image verified.” Use streaming SHA-256 in bounded blocks and record member hashes for multi-file acquisitions. A changed source becomes a separate revision; annotations keep the hash and selector they were created against.

For local saves, use SQLite transactions and rollback-journal mode; on startup run integrity checks, recover the last valid DB and offer a previous generation if needed. Bundle creation uses a new versioned staging directory or archive: copy the SQLite DB through SQLite’s backup mechanism, validate it, include manifest/schema and required derived assets, hash all members, then mark/publish a completed generation. Retain the prior valid generation. Do not assume an atomic rename on a shared storage server: write a unique generation and a completion manifest last, validate on copy, and test interruption/recovery on the actual university filesystem. Only closed, completed bundles are exchanged; no simultaneous multi-host database edits are supported.

Default bundles reference external sources to avoid duplicating multi-gigabyte files. Store original URI, project-relative path if available, basename, size, content hash, and group/series selection. On relocation, search by recorded relative path/name/size as a hint, then hash-verify before relinking. An optional “collect source files” action makes a portable bundle but warns about required storage, estimates size, verifies copied hashes and supports cancel/resume. Portability costs disk and copy time; external references save space but rely on file availability and exact bytes. Neither mode modifies the source. Derived cache and masks are clearly marked and may be regenerated; original metadata and source identity are not replaced by those derivatives.

## Export contract

- **Annotation table:** UTF-8 CSV plus a JSON schema/sidecar. Include annotation/image IDs; source SHA-256 and group/series; source array axes/order/shape/dtype; coordinate convention; index vertices and explicit axis names; calibrated world coordinates and units if known; plane/time/channel selectors and scope; geometry type; style ID; source transform and derivation IDs. Use explicit null/unknown values. Polygon vertices must not be reduced to a bounding box. The sidecar carries the full affine, operation history and export software/version.
- **Overlay:** optional OME-Zarr v0.5 integer label image for mask annotations. For point/polygon overlays, rasterization is a new derived label image; store a mapping from integer label IDs to annotation UUIDs and label properties, and state that rasterization loses vector precision and can have overlap/rasterization policies. Keep the annotation table as authoritative geometry. Include source hash and image path/selector in the sidecar/label source metadata. Do not claim that the overlay alone preserves all styles or transforms.
- **Small derived image:** allow a native-sample crop/plane copy that preserves source dtype and sample values when no resampling is requested, with explicit axes/calibration metadata if representable and a sidecar linking source hash, crop bounds, mapping and checksum. Any resampling, orientation bake-in or dtype change is a distinct recorded derivation with matrix, output grid, interpolation, boundary rule, dtype conversion, operator and output hash. An 8-bit rendered PNG is a display product: record selected channels/composite, LUT, window/level, projection/slice/time, scaling, orientation and that it does not preserve native precision or all metadata. Never label it a lossless scientific copy.

## Support boundary, opportunity and alternative

**Minimum support choice.** Whitelist OME-Zarr v0.5 and a small, fixture-backed set of TIFF/BigTIFF/OME-TIFF patterns read through one pinned `tifffile`/codec/Zarr stack. Require recognized series and axes, known pixel type, explainable calibration, and a bounded plane/ROI read path. Fail without data mutation for unsupported codecs, corrupt/contradictory metadata, unknown axes, excessive chunk/plane size, or unknown file families; preserve the project, source locator/raw metadata, and import failure details. For formats not on the list, provide a documented conversion/import option in a later stage rather than claiming they work.

**Opportunity.** Add a user-chosen Bio-Formats conversion adapter after the allowlist is stable. Its broad format coverage may help accommodate instrument diversity, while its per-format metadata/export ratings give a concrete basis for choosing a narrow list [S3]. A conversion to chunked OME-Zarr could create bounded-access derivatives, but does not by itself prove bounded memory or preserve every source field. Keep the original file, conversion recipe/version, warnings, raw metadata, output hash and tests.

**Plausible alternative.** A custom Qt + VisPy canvas and `ArrayAccess` layer could avoid adopting napari internals and give the team direct control over lazy reads and coordinate mapping. It would cost substantially more work for image navigation, annotation tools, keyboard bindings, accessibility and undo/redo; select it only if napari’s integration tests fail on required interaction or performance. Do not build a full viewer in this experiment. Multi-user live editing, cloud sync, registration and automatic segmentation remain optional future directions, not MVP obligations. Registration would need its own explicit transform/lineage model; automatic masks need human review and do not replace exact provenance.

## Proposed validation (all UNEXECUTED unless W1)

1. **W1 — executed component check:** a pure-Python coordinate formula and display-window demonstration is recorded in `witnesses.json`. It confirms arithmetic for one anisotropic scale/translation example and that this sample view function left a small source list unchanged. It does not exercise an image reader, the NGFF implementation, napari, persistence, export, 3 GB data or a complete application.
2. **W2 — 3 GB bounded access / responsiveness, proposed UNEXECUTED:** on the 8-core/16 GB target machine, use a known 3 GB tiled TIFF and a chunked OME-Zarr specimen; log adapter calls, bytes/chunks read, decode concurrency, process RSS, latency and cancellation. Pan/zoom, flip through `z`/`t`, change visible channels and edit a mask. Assert navigation never materializes the full source, cache stays within its configured bound, no whole-stack contrast scan occurs, cancel returns control and progress advances. Compare a deliberately reshaped Dask view with axis-aware slicing because napari’s guide documents severe read amplification [S5]; test direct Zarr and Dask-wrapped Zarr because issue #9515 reports a difference [S6]. Proposed product targets for a first benchmark: decoded cache ≤512 MiB, at most two in-flight decode requests, warm cached-plane navigation p95 ≤150 ms, annotation edit/undo p95 ≤100 ms, cancel acknowledgement ≤250 ms. Revise from measured workstation evidence; these are not vendor guarantees.
3. **W3 — axes/calibration/coordinates, proposed UNEXECUTED:** synthesize `TCZYX`, `CZYX`, `ZYX` and `YX` datasets with axis permutations, anisotropic spacing, nonzero origin, known physical units, flips and rotated affine. Check display→source-index→world and inverse round trips at voxel centers and fractional points; confirm nonselected T/C/Z values remain explicit; open/close on another machine and compare coordinates. Include unknown unit, custom axis, missing origin and contradictory metadata. Require preserved raw metadata and deliberate correction prompts.
4. **W4 — edits/transform regression, proposed UNEXECUTED:** mirror the RGB split issue fix with nontrivial scale, translate, affine, rotate/shear and channel permutations; test that layer operations, mask edits, derived view and resampling preserve or explicitly change the matrix as specified. Add save/reopen/export tests, which the cited napari regression did not cover [S7–S11].
5. **W5 — save/import/export interruption and relocation, proposed UNEXECUTED:** inject failures during source hashing, metadata parse, cache write, each DB transaction, bundle copy, completion-manifest write and export. After restart, prove the prior generation still opens and no partial image/export is reported complete. Move/rename the source; test match, same-path/different-hash and missing-source states. On the actual shared storage, test interrupted bundle copying/rename and validate recovery markers; do not infer network filesystem guarantees from local SQLite behavior [S12–S13].
6. **W6 — export truth and usability, proposed UNEXECUTED:** round-trip point/polygon/mask across plane, volume and whole-acquisition scopes; compare exported table coordinates and UUIDs; render overlay and verify label mapping, overlaps and source hash; crop without resampling and compare output dtype/sample values to source; then resample and verify operation ledger/output checksum. Test warning text for PNG precision loss, keyboard-only channel/slice/time/tool/undo/redo, focus order, and legibility/announcing of axis/unit/error state.

The staged exit gate is source-correct coordinate and file identity, bounded reads under the memory target, recoverable project and honest export metadata. Until W2–W6 pass on the locked package matrix and shared storage, the 3 GB target, cross-machine recovery and package compatibility remain engineering assumptions, not accomplished behavior.
